1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,249
Every dashboard starts with a question
that someone has to translate into a tool.

2
00:00:06,249 --> 00:00:08,619
Semaphore skips the translation.

3
00:00:08,619 --> 00:00:13,545
You describe the page in plain
English, you review what it drafts,

4
00:00:13,545 --> 00:00:18,173
and leadership asks questions
that your reviewed work answers.

5
00:00:18,173 --> 00:00:20,543
All of it runs on this computer.

6
00:00:20,793 --> 00:00:23,870
Semaphore is a dashboard
workbench for analysts,

7
00:00:23,870 --> 00:00:26,050
and a question box for leadership.

8
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:29,325
Three pieces: a launcher, a small server

9
00:00:29,325 --> 00:00:32,272
on this computer, and the web page.

10
00:00:32,272 --> 00:00:35,930
Double-click the launcher,
and it starts the server,

11
00:00:35,930 --> 00:00:38,814
which hands your browser a one-time link.

12
00:00:39,583 --> 00:00:41,851
The server listens on loopback
only, and keeps hoists,

13
00:00:41,851 --> 00:00:44,497
the catalog and the playbook as
plain files in one home folder.

14
00:00:45,197 --> 00:00:49,373
Halyard feeds it backlog health as
four CSV files, read in the browser.

15
00:00:49,373 --> 00:00:55,352
PacketRat and Eight Bells exports load the
same way, tested so far on sample files.

16
00:00:56,052 --> 00:01:00,746
For drafting, the server calls Fairlead
with its own key from Credential Manager.

17
00:01:00,746 --> 00:01:05,423
Fairlead routes to the model, and
gets its model keys from Lazarette.

18
00:01:05,423 --> 00:01:07,392
The page never sees a key.

19
00:01:08,092 --> 00:01:11,929
Databases and the data lake
use Semaphore's own connectors,

20
00:01:11,929 --> 00:01:13,620
and still need validation.

21
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:16,463
Routing them through Fairlead is planned.

22
00:01:17,074 --> 00:01:21,603
Safety: one read-only select per query,
an analyst accepts every draft,

23
00:01:21,603 --> 00:01:24,282
and leadership sees reviewed answers only.

24
00:01:24,282 --> 00:01:25,696
Semaphore ships as one folder.

25
00:01:25,696 --> 00:01:28,346
Download the zip, unblock it,

26
00:01:28,346 --> 00:01:32,642
extract it, and double-click
Launch-Semaphore.

27
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:35,039
Nothing to install, no admin rights.

28
00:01:35,739 --> 00:01:39,611
It starts a small server on this
computer, on a loopback port,

29
00:01:39,611 --> 00:01:42,546
and gives your browser
a one-time session link.

30
00:01:42,546 --> 00:01:44,806
Nothing leaves the machine.

31
00:01:45,506 --> 00:01:50,691
Four ways in: continue the example,
a new page, your own data,

32
00:01:50,691 --> 00:01:52,615
or the leadership view.

33
00:01:52,865 --> 00:01:55,296
Models come through Fairlead.

34
00:01:55,296 --> 00:01:58,417
Its OpenAI key sits in
Lazarette, the vault,

35
00:01:58,417 --> 00:02:01,113
and Fairlead only sees what you share.

36
00:02:01,813 --> 00:02:04,933
On Fairlead's Clients page,
pick Semaphore and install.

37
00:02:04,933 --> 00:02:09,949
The key lands in Windows Credential
Manager, and the page never holds it.

38
00:02:10,649 --> 00:02:12,879
A config file picks the route.

39
00:02:12,879 --> 00:02:17,127
This demo data is synthetic,
so it uses the smart route.

40
00:02:17,127 --> 00:02:21,627
Remove these two lines and drafts stay
on the local route, on this computer.

41
00:02:21,877 --> 00:02:24,393
This is a hoist: one page of flags.

42
00:02:24,393 --> 00:02:27,060
The brief across the top is plain English,

43
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:29,853
and Semaphore highlights
what it understood.

44
00:02:30,724 --> 00:02:32,842
Click Gulf and the whole page filters.

45
00:02:33,542 --> 00:02:35,559
The monthly trend ignores it on purpose.

46
00:02:36,259 --> 00:02:39,596
Flip a flag over to read what
it shows in plain English,

47
00:02:39,596 --> 00:02:41,742
and the exact SQL behind the number.

48
00:02:42,358 --> 00:02:46,563
The AI switch is on, so
drafts go through Fairlead.

49
00:02:46,563 --> 00:02:51,032
Describe a flag in plain English:
top ten hulls by labor cost.

50
00:02:51,732 --> 00:02:54,479
A few seconds later:
a table, a confidence figure,

51
00:02:54,479 --> 00:02:56,457
and a note that Fairlead drafted it.

52
00:02:56,457 --> 00:03:00,040
Every key the model used was
checked against the catalog.

53
00:03:00,740 --> 00:03:03,701
Accept it, and the playbook keeps it.

54
00:03:03,701 --> 00:03:06,880
Brief me reads the page back,
every sentence tied to a flag.

55
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:11,359
Halyard writes backlog health
for Semaphore as four CSV files.

56
00:03:12,059 --> 00:03:14,391
This is the real export
from the Halyard demo.

57
00:03:15,091 --> 00:03:16,910
Add them under Data.

58
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:21,463
Semaphore recognises Halyard's schema
and proposes measures and fields:

59
00:03:21,463 --> 00:03:24,092
thirty-four measures, sixty-three fields.

60
00:03:24,792 --> 00:03:25,977
Now describe flags.

61
00:03:25,977 --> 00:03:29,428
Items needing review: seven.

62
00:03:29,428 --> 00:03:34,519
Items by type: twenty stories,
seven tasks, four features.

63
00:03:34,519 --> 00:03:39,968
Items by state. Features by status:
three on track, one at risk.

64
00:03:39,968 --> 00:03:43,187
Sprint velocity, planned against done.

65
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:45,749
A person accepts each draft.

66
00:03:45,749 --> 00:03:49,176
Accepted flags are what
leadership can ask about.

67
00:03:49,876 --> 00:03:55,656
In the Ask view, the same numbers
answer questions, labelled as reviewed.

68
00:03:55,970 --> 00:03:59,420
The decision: can we
commit the next sprint?

69
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:04,736
Four numbers decide it: items needing
review, ready stories, blockers,

70
00:04:04,736 --> 00:04:06,331
and features at risk.

71
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:09,319
First, the raw export in PowerShell.

72
00:04:09,319 --> 00:04:13,148
Four commands, typed at a steady
pace: about eleven seconds.

73
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:22,056
Now Semaphore, same four
questions, same typing speed.

74
00:04:22,056 --> 00:04:25,241
One import, four plain-English briefs,

75
00:04:25,241 --> 00:04:28,258
four accepts: about twenty seconds.

76
00:04:28,258 --> 00:04:31,269
For one analyst and four
numbers, PowerShell is faster.

77
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:44,600
But now a leader can ask
the question themselves,

78
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:48,522
and the answer comes back in
under a second, labelled reviewed.

79
00:04:49,222 --> 00:04:51,521
The answer: not yet.

80
00:04:51,521 --> 00:04:54,469
Nothing is ready, seven items need review,

81
00:04:54,469 --> 00:04:57,627
there is one blocker
and one feature at risk.

82
00:04:57,627 --> 00:05:00,443
Sample data: the Halyard demo backlog.

83
00:05:00,443 --> 00:05:02,975
Semaphore reads more than CSV.

84
00:05:02,975 --> 00:05:08,464
Here is what connects, and
how well each one is proven.

85
00:05:08,899 --> 00:05:14,588
In the browser: CSV, Halyard exports,
PacketRat and Eight Bells exports,

86
00:05:14,588 --> 00:05:16,326
and Qlik load scripts.

87
00:05:16,326 --> 00:05:21,178
A QVD is Qlik's own format,
so export it to CSV first.

88
00:05:21,498 --> 00:05:26,829
Through the server: databases over ODBC
or OLE DB, including Postgres on AWS RDS,

89
00:05:26,829 --> 00:05:28,803
Oracle, SQL Server and Access.

90
00:05:28,803 --> 00:05:32,539
Plus an S3 data lake through
Athena, with a cost guard.

91
00:05:32,539 --> 00:05:36,502
The AWS paths are built and
tested against simulated AWS.

92
00:05:36,502 --> 00:05:38,062
Live validation is pending.

93
00:05:38,420 --> 00:05:40,509
Here is a real database.

94
00:05:40,509 --> 00:05:43,700
This Access file holds
the Halyard backlog.

95
00:05:43,700 --> 00:05:47,978
One entry in sources.json, then
pick the table, profile and add.

96
00:05:48,563 --> 00:05:52,134
Now the same plain-English brief runs

97
00:05:52,134 --> 00:05:55,318
a real query through the server,

98
00:05:55,318 --> 00:05:57,345
and the SQL is shown.

99
00:05:57,595 --> 00:06:03,337
Share as a report saves one HTML file with
working filters, summarised numbers only,

100
00:06:03,337 --> 00:06:05,798
optionally locked with a passphrase.

101
00:06:06,498 --> 00:06:09,473
It also reads PacketRat and Eight Bells

102
00:06:09,473 --> 00:06:12,754
exports, tested so far on synthetic files.

103
00:06:13,254 --> 00:06:14,854
Semaphore drafts.

104
00:06:14,854 --> 00:06:16,171
People review.

105
00:06:16,171 --> 00:06:17,677
Leadership asks.

106
00:06:17,677 --> 00:06:23,570
And a thought for your team:
before you rent a dashboard tool,

107
00:06:23,570 --> 00:06:26,897
ask what you could build in a week.

108
00:06:26,897 --> 00:06:30,943
The new frontier is
compute and creativity.

109
00:06:30,943 --> 00:06:32,543
That's the watch.

110
00:06:32,543 --> 00:06:33,578
Fair winds.
